# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# CACHING RULES FOR CLOUDFLARE PAGES.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS.
# Pages caches static assets by file type. HTML is served with
# max-age=0 and revalidated on every visit, which is right. But
# rabbits.js is a .js file, so it was getting the default:
#
#     cache-control: public, max-age=14400, must-revalidate
#
# Four hours, in the visitor's own browser. rabbits.js is the ONE
# file on this site whose whole job is to be accurate today. Two
# rabbits were marked reserved and the site kept telling returning
# visitors they were available, because their browser had a copy from
# earlier and would not ask again for four hours. Someone enquires
# about a rabbit that is already spoken for, and the site looks
# either careless or dishonest. Neither is recoverable in one email.
#
# max-age=0 with must-revalidate does NOT mean re-downloading it
# every time. The file still carries an ETag, so the browser asks
# "has this changed?" and gets a 304 with no body when it hasn't.
# The cost is one round trip; the benefit is that availability is
# never wrong.
#
# Photographs are deliberately left alone. A rabbit's photo does not
# change after it is published, so the long default cache is right
# for those and keeps the site fast.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

/rabbits.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Same reasoning: this page carries what is available and what has
# been placed, and it is cheap to revalidate.
/litters.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
